          <div class="markdown"><span><p><strong>Data</strong> <strong>dictionary</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>PassengerId</strong>: This is a unique identifier assigned to each passenger. It's an integer and doesn't directly contribute to survival prediction but is useful for identification.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Survived</strong>: This is the target variable. It's a binary indicator where <code>0</code> means the passenger did <em>not</em> survive, and <code>1</code> means the passenger <em>did</em> survive.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Pclass</strong>: This represents the passenger's ticket class, indicating socio-economic status. It's an ordinal categorical variable with three possible values: <code>1</code> (1st class), <code>2</code> (2nd class), and <code>3</code> (3rd class).</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Name</strong>: The name of the passenger. This is a string (object type) and can be useful for feature engineering (e.g., extracting titles like 'Mr.', 'Mrs.').</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Sex</strong>: The sex of the passenger (male or female). This is a categorical variable.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Age</strong>: The age of the passenger in years. This is a numerical variable. It has missing values, which will need to be handled during data preprocessing.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>SibSp</strong>: This indicates the number of siblings or spouses accompanying the passenger on board. This is an integer.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Parch</strong>: This indicates the number of parents or children accompanying the passenger on board. This is an integer.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Ticket</strong>: The ticket number. This is a string (object type) and might contain patterns that could be useful, though often it's too varied to be directly used without complex feature engineering.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Fare</strong>: The fare paid by the passenger. This is a numerical variable (float).</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Cabin</strong>: The cabin number. This column has a significant number of missing values and is a string (object type). It might indicate the deck level, which could be relevant to survival.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Embarked</strong>: The port where the passenger embarked. This is a categorical variable (string/object type) with values like 'C' (Cherbourg), 'Q' (Queenstown), or 'S' (Southampton).</p>
</li>
</ul></span></div>
